## 3. How to Connect SSH to the Active VNC Desktop

When you log in via SSH, your terminal lands on a login node (e.g., `grace5`). Your Open OnDemand VNC desktop runs on an allocated GPU compute node (e.g., `r001`).

### Step 3.1: Locate your active GPU node
```bash
squeue -u $USER
```
Look at the `NODELIST` column (for example, `r001`).

### Step 3.2: SSH into the GPU node
```bash
ssh r001
```

### Step 3.3: Detect your TurboVNC Display
Run:
```bash
ps -u $USER -o pid,cmd | grep -E "Xvnc|Xorg"
```
You will see output similar to:
```text
3902363 /sw/hprc/sw/ood/TurboVNC/bin/Xvnc :2 -desktop TurboVNC: r001:2 ...
```
Note the display number (here `:2`). Export the display and authority in your shell:
```bash
export DISPLAY=:2
export XAUTHORITY=/home/jacob.dannie.am/.Xauthority
```

